==============================================================================
MECHANISM TEST -- pre-registered: gain must concentrate on low-vis / freezing conditions
==============================================================================
Each row also reports its SHARE OF THE TOTAL REALIZED GAIN (not an
isolated hypothetical) -- for the three complementary pairs below
(low_vis/VFR, freezing, deicing) each pair sums to ~100% by
construction, since they partition all rows. A pre-registered
mechanism claim needs the ADVERSE side to dominate this share, not
just have the larger per-row delta -- a huge per-row effect on a
tiny population can still lose the share to a tiny per-row effect
on the 80-99% majority. Report both; don't call it a clean pass
on the per-row delta alone (PROGRESS.md S24/S25).
  low_vis (IFR/MVFR/LIFR)    n=68510    rows= 19.9%  base=  482.4  treat=  479.3  delta=   -3.1  share_of_total_gain= 51.1%
  VFR (ordinary)             n=275829   rows= 80.1%  base=  290.7  treat=  289.5  delta=   -1.2  share_of_total_gain= 48.9%
  below_freezing             n=13660    rows=  4.0%  base=  302.3  treat=  293.0  delta=   -9.3  share_of_total_gain= 18.9%
  above freezing             n=330679   rows= 96.0%  base=  339.0  treat=  337.6  delta=   -1.5  share_of_total_gain= 81.1%
  deicing_risk               n=1321     rows=  0.4%  base=  575.0  treat=  540.8  delta=  -34.1  share_of_total_gain= 12.6%
  no deicing_risk            n=343018   rows= 99.6%  base=  336.4  treat=  334.9  delta=   -1.5  share_of_total_gain= 87.4%

A uniform-looking per-row delta (both sides negative/positive by a similar
amount) means the gain isn't concentrated and shouldn't be read as weather
signal, same logic that rejected the queue feature (S23). But even a correctly
concentrated per-row delta can still have most of its AGGREGATE share come from
the majority population -- that's not a mechanism failure, just an honest
accounting of where the realized RMSE gain actually comes from.
